  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "For the functions $f(x)$ and $p(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(-3)=-6,\\,{f}^{\u2032}(-3)=2,\\,p(-3)=-5,\\,{p}^{\u2032}(-3)=-3\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+p(x))$ at $x=-3.$",
  "worked_solution": "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+p(x))={f}^{\u2032}(x)+{p}^{\u2032}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-3$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)+p(x))|}_{x=-3} & ={f}^{\u2032}(-3)+{p}^{\u2032}(-3) \\\\  & =2+(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
